Source pages (all HTTP 200, non-preapproved sources, so quotes are kept short):
- https://aistudio.google.com/docs/image-understanding.md (first 39,932 of 51,266 chars read verbatim; the rest is a model summary)
- https://aistudio.google.com/docs/structured-output.md (first 39,934 of 61,442 chars verbatim; rest is a model summary)
- https://aistudio.google.com/docs/interactions-overview.md (full page, verbatim)

## 1. Inline base64 JPEG + text prompt

From the image-understanding page, JavaScript "Passing inline image data" example (verbatim, trimmed):

```ts
import { GoogleGenAI } from "@google/genai";
import * as fs from "node:fs";

const client = new GoogleGenAI({});
const base64ImageFile = fs.readFileSync("path/to/small-sample.jpg", {
  encoding: "base64",
});

const interaction = await client.interactions.create({
    model: "gemini-4-hydrogen",
    input: [
        {type: "text", text: "Caption this image."},
        {
            type: "image",
            data: base64ImageFile,
            mime_type: "image/jpeg"
        }
    ]
});
console.log(interaction.output_text);
```

- `input` is an array of content parts. Image part fields: `type: "image"`, `data` (base64 string, no `data:` prefix in the example), `mime_type` (snake_case, `"image/jpeg"`).
- Read the text with `interaction.output_text`.
- Doc note: inline data limits total request size (text, system instructions, bytes) to 20MB; use the File API (`client.files.upload`, then `uri` + `mime_type`) for larger files.
- Not from the verbatim text: the model-extracted summary says the page advises putting the text prompt before the image for a single image. Every doc example uses that order.

## 2. JSON response matching a JSON schema

The parameter is `response_format` (not `responseSchema`, `response_json_schema` or `config`). Verbatim from the structured-output page, JavaScript recipe example, trimmed:

```ts
const interaction = await client.interactions.create({
  model: "gemini-4-hydrogen",
  input: prompt,
  response_format: {
    type: 'text',
    mime_type: 'application/json',
    schema: recipeJsonSchema
  },
});

const recipe = recipeSchema.parse(JSON.parse(interaction.output_text));
```

- `recipeJsonSchema` is a plain JSON Schema object (`type: "object"`, `properties`, `required`).
- Read it with `JSON.parse(interaction.output_text)`. Zod is optional (`z.fromJSONSchema(schema)` to build a validator; `z.toJSONSchema(zodObj)` to produce the schema from Zod).
- Illustrative shape of a schema with the image input (my example, not from the docs):

```ts
response_format: {
  type: "text",
  mime_type: "application/json",
  schema: {
    type: "object",
    properties: { label: { type: "string" } },
    required: ["label"]
  }
}
```

- Image-understanding page (object detection) passes the same `response_format` next to an image part in `input`.

## 3. Thinking / latency

- Verbatim from the image-understanding page (segmentation example): `generation_config: { thinking_level: "high" }`. This is a sibling of `response_format` in the `create` call.
- Doc callout (verbatim): "Gemini 4 Hydrogen defaults to `thinking_level` set to `"high"`. For lower latency on high-volume segmentation, you can also test `"low"`, or use `"minimal"` on models that support minimal thinking ... such as `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`."
- Interactions overview says `generation_config` (including `thinking_level`, `temperature`) is interaction-scoped and must be re-sent per call.
- Not verified: I did not fetch the thinking page (`/docs/thinking#thinking-levels`), so I can't confirm which models accept `"minimal"`, or whether a full disable exists. Fetch that page if you need it.

```ts
generation_config: { thinking_level: "low" }
```

## 4. Version and apiVersion

- Interactions overview (verbatim): "On JavaScript, this is `@google/genai` package from `2.3.0` version onwards."
- No `apiVersion` requirement is stated for the JS SDK. The REST examples send the header `Api-Revision: 2026-05-20` to `https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/interactions`. The SDK snippets do not set it.

## Notes
- The docs use `model: "gemini-4-hydrogen"` throughout. Other IDs listed on the overview page include `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`, `gemini-3.8-flash`, `gemini-3.6-flash`, `gemini-2.5-flash`.
- Overview page: `store` defaults to `true`. Set `store: false` to opt out. This is incompatible with `background=true` and with `previous_interaction_id`.

